#{[17592186507424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507424 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c1ae5e1-e0dd-5c7b-a210-a9ab2fb7f3cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507424 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2}+{z}^{2}=(\\sqrt{8})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point point $C(1,-1,0)$ and the radius $r=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the radius of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2}=24?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2a5a0df-a4bf-58c3-8d59-4e8c846dde92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507505 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =24+1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}={5}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a radius of $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+(z-1)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8885925-a2a0-5076-82d9-35a02e8077ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507511 #edb/ref 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+(z-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcd64bf8-3618-5eb7-9b5b-cffee6a7498a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507515 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(0)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e843728c-761f-5357-930a-f6a6507b4966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507523 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{4}{8} & =1 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,\\frac{3}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{5}{2},0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
